      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-16935",
        "knowledge_point_id": "0236acfd-59d1-5bc8-9e60-1c76ddc536f5",
        "knowledge_point": "Finding a Quadratic Equation Given a Complex Root and Its Leading Coefficient",
        "problem": "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $4$, find the equation.",
        "worked_solution": "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=\\frac{1}{2}-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))(x-(\\frac{1}{2}-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(\\frac{1}{2}-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})-x(\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})+(\\frac{1}{2}+\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})(\\frac{1}{2}-\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{x}{2}+x\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}-\\frac{x}{2}-x\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}+\\frac{1}{4}-3{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}+3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+\\frac{13}{4} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $4.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}-x+\\frac{13}{4}) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-4x+13 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
