        "problem": "If $x=y-2{y}^{3},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at the point $(1,-1).$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y-2{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =1-6{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1-6{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(1,-1),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(1,-1)} & =\\frac{1}{1-6(-1)^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
