        "problem": "$P(15,4)=$",
        "worked_solution": "The formula for ${}_{n}{P}_{r}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}\\,.\n$$\n\n$P(15,4)$ means the same thing as ${}_{15}{P}_{4}.$ Using the usual formula for ${}_{n}{P}_{r},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(15,4) & =\\frac{15!}{(15-4)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{15!}{11!} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\cdot 14\\cdot 13\\cdot 12\\cdot \\cancel{11!}}{\\cancel{11!}} \\\\  & =15\\cdot 14\\cdot 13\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =32\\,760.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
